Ask UHD for a receive socket buffer, instead of raising a limit nothing uses.

Measured on DOB 2026-08-11:

    netstat -su
        1859745252 packet receive errors
        RcvbufErrors: 1859745252

1.86 billion UDP datagrams discarded because the receive socket queue was
full. At 1472 bytes each that is **2.7 TB against 41 TB received -- about 6% of
every recording**, continuously, for as long as the machine had been up. After
this change the counter stopped moving entirely.

Nothing reported it. `ethtool -S` was clean, `ip -s link` showed zero drops,
the link was 1000Mb/s full duplex, and 27 billion packets had arrived without
a single CRC or framing error. Socket-queue overflows appear in **none** of
those places -- only in `netstat -su` / `/proc/net/snmp`, which is why this
went unnoticed. If you are ever chasing loss between a USRP and an
application, check that counter before the network.

The station already had the two settings that are usually blamed:

    net.core.rmem_max = 500000000        # set
    ethtool -g enp0s25 -> RX: 4096       # set

Neither does anything on its own. `rmem_max` is a *ceiling* on what a process
may request; UHD still has to ask, and it asks via `recv_buff_size` in
`--usrp_args`. Without it UHD takes its own modest default and the raised
ceiling is never approached. The ring size governs the NIC's DMA, which was
never the constraint here -- `rx_missed_errors` totalled 68,666 over five days,
about one second of samples.

`chirp-rx.service` has always passed this. `dombas.sh` never has, which is why
the station has been quietly losing 6% while the systemd unit sitting unused
beside it would not have.

**Verifying.** UHD prints the request at startup and complains loudly if the
kernel refuses it:

    grep -iE 'recv_buff|could not be resized' logs/thor.log | head

You want `Creating the USRP device with: recv_buff_size=500000000` and *no*
"The recv buffer could not be resized sufficiently" warning. If that warning
appears, `net.core.rmem_max` is lower than the request and the sysctl above is
the missing half.

Then confirm the counter has stopped:

    netstat -su | grep RcvbufErrors; sleep 30; netstat -su | grep RcvbufErrors

Two identical numbers is the result. The absolute value never falls -- it is
cumulative since boot.

**This does not fix the `D` overflow markers in thor.log.** Those are a
different mechanism: `ERROR_CODE_OVERFLOW` means the *device* discarded
samples because the host did not call `recv()` fast enough, so the packets
were never transmitted and no network counter can see them. That fault was
still present at ~1500 events/s after this change.

Its cause was established by removing the competition rather than by guessing:
with `detect_chirps`, `calc_ionograms`, the five digisonde receivers, the
plotters and `find_timings` all killed, leaving only the recorder and
`drf ringbuffer`, the rate went to **exactly zero over a 60 s window**. It is
CPU contention, and it needs core isolation rather than another tuning flag.
See `docs/2026-08-11-recorder-packet-loss.md`.

Apply on top of 0003 (which this context assumes) and 0004:

    cd ~/chirpsounder2
    git apply --check /path/to/0005-dombas-set-usrp-recv-buff-size.patch
    git apply         /path/to/0005-dombas-set-usrp-recv-buff-size.patch

Takes effect on the next recorder start. `dombas.sh` restarts the recorder
every 24 hours in a loop, so a full relaunch is not strictly required -- but
the 24-hour boundary is an unpredictable moment to change behaviour, and a
deliberate relaunch is easier to attribute a measurement to.

`addr0=` is deliberately omitted. `chirp-rx.service` hardcodes
`addr0=192.168.10.2`; discovery already finds the device without it, and a
wrong address turns a tuning change into a station that cannot open its radio.

diff --git a/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh b/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
--- a/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
+++ b/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
@@ -74,7 +74,13 @@ echo "rx_uhd_ext_gps (restarting every 24 hours)"
     while true;
     do
         # TBD: change cpp program so that ini file defines USRP setup!
-        ./rx_uhd_ext_gps --outdir="$RINGBUFFER_DIR" > logs/thor.log 2>&1
+        # recv_buff_size is what UHD actually requests; net.core.rmem_max only
+        # permits it. Without this the socket queue overflowed 1.86 billion
+        # times -- 6% of every recording -- while ethtool, ip -s link and the
+        # link status all read perfectly clean. Only netstat -su showed it.
+        ./rx_uhd_ext_gps --outdir="$RINGBUFFER_DIR" \
+            --usrp_args=recv_buff_size=500000000 \
+            > logs/thor.log 2>&1

         sleep 5

